BT
/F1 9 Tf
11 TL
40 760 Td
(Gladstone Fire Department EMS Protocols - 2026 Revision V3) Tj
T*
(Text-rendered internal source copy | Original PDF physical page 4 of 142) Tj
T*
T*
(7/25/2023) Tj
T*
(PROFESSIONALISM) Tj
T*
(EMS is really all about service. People like us, who have chosen to serve in EMS, have a) Tj
T*
(moral and) Tj
T*
(ethical obligation to behave in ways that serve the needs of others and reflect positively) Tj
T*
(upon our) Tj
T*
(organization, our community, and the whole discipline of EMS. This is not easy. In fact,) Tj
T*
(it's a constant) Tj
T*
(struggle that takes significant time, dedication, and effort. The choices we make and the) Tj
T*
(manner in which) Tj
T*
(we behave are a constant reflection of our professionalism. The following paragraphs were) Tj
T*
(taken from Dr.) Tj
T*
(Brain Bledsoe's "Paramedic Emergency Care Second Edition," and establish the foundation that) Tj
T*
(all) Tj
T*
(providers \(volunteer or career\) should strive to achieve and maintain.) Tj
T*
("Professionalism describes the conduct or qualities that characterize a practitioner in a) Tj
T*
(particular) Tj
T*
(field or occupation. Health care professionals promote quality patient care and take pride) Tj
T*
(in their) Tj
T*
(profession. They earn the respect and confidence of team members by performing their duties) Tj
T*
(to) Tj
T*
(the best of their abilities and by exhibiting a high level of respect for their profession.) Tj
T*
(Attaining) Tj
T*
(professionalism is not easy. It requires an understanding of what distinguishes the) Tj
T*
(professional) Tj
T*
(from the non-professional. To develop this skill, keep the following points in mind.) Tj
T*
(Professionals) Tj
T*
(place the patient first; non-professionals place their egos first. Professionals practice) Tj
T*
(their skills) Tj
T*
(to the point of mastery, and then keep practicing them to improve and remain sharp.) Tj
T*
(Nonprofessionals do not believe their skills will fade and see no reason to constantly) Tj
T*
(strive for) Tj
T*
(improvement. Professionals understand the importance of response times; non-professionals) Tj
T*
(get) Tj
T*
(to an accident when it's convenient. Professionals take refresher courses seriously, because) Tj
T*
(they) Tj
T*
(know they have forgotten a lot and because they are eager for new information.) Tj
T*
(Non-professionals) Tj
T*
(believe they don't need training sessions and dislike being required to attend them.) Tj
T*
(Professionals) Tj
T*
(critically review their performance, always seeking a way to improve. Non-professionals look) Tj
T*
(to) Tj
T*
(protect themselves, to hide inadequacies, and to place blame on others. Professionals check) Tj
T*
(out) Tj
T*
(their equipment prior to the emergency response. Non-professionals hope that everything will) Tj
T*
(work, supplies will be in place, batteries will be charged, and oxygen levels will be) Tj
T*
(adequate.) Tj
T*
(Maintaining professionalism requires effort. But, the result of that effort - the admiration) Tj
T*
(and) Tj
T*
(respect of one's peers - is the highest compliment a person can receive.") Tj
T*
(The NHTSA's National Standard Curriculum lists the following as professional behaviors:) Tj
T*
(* Integrity) Tj
T*
(* Empathy) Tj
T*
(* Self-motivation) Tj
T*
(* Appearance and personal hygiene) Tj
T*
(* Self-confidence) Tj
T*
(* Communications) Tj
T*
(* Time management) Tj
T*
(* Teamwork and diplomacy) Tj
T*
(* Respect) Tj
T*
(* Patient advocacy) Tj
T*
(* Careful delivery of services.) Tj
T*
(Being a professional has nothing to do with pay or rank or the level of certification that) Tj
T*
(you hold. It is) Tj
T*
(the goal that every member of our practice, from EMT-Basic to the Medical Director, to) Tj
T*
(constantly) Tj
T*
(strives for and remain a comprehensive, clinically sophisticated, and compassionate EMS) Tj
T*
T*
