(1. Known esophageal disease \(varicies, alcoholism, cirrhosis etc.\).) Tj
T*
(2. Known ingestion of a caustic substance.) Tj
T*
(3. Patients who are conscious or who have an intact gag reflex.) Tj
T*
(If the patient regains consciousness or begins to fight the tube) Tj
T*
(1. You may remove the King Tube.) Tj
T*
(CAUTION: When removing the King Tube there is a strong possibility of emesis.) Tj
T*
(2. You may leave the King Tube in place and sedate the patient.) Tj
T*
(Endotracheal intubation) Tj
T*
(1. Endotracheal intubation provides a definitive airway. Every attempt) Tj
T*
(following protocol should be made to secure the airway with an) Tj
T*
(Endotracheal tube.) Tj
T*
(a. If the King Tube has been placed prior to your arrival and is functioning) Tj
T*
(properly, the decision to leave it in place should be highly considered.) Tj
T*
(b. If deemed necessary by ALS personnel, an endotracheal tube may be) Tj
T*
(inserted to take the place of the King Tube.) Tj
T*
(Additional notes) Tj
T*
(1. Placement of the King Tube should never delay:) Tj
T*
(a. CPR.) Tj
T*
(b. Basic airway management.) Tj
T*
(c. Use of the defibrillation unit.) Tj
T*
(d. Other necessary patient care.) Tj
T*
(2. Adequate ventilation should be assessed by the use of:) Tj
T*
(a. Standard checks for breath sounds and visual chest rise.) Tj
T*
(b. Capnography.) Tj
T*
(c. Pulse oximetry if available.) Tj
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
